WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:10.230
In the last few years, the pace of progress
in machine learning research has been staggering.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.909
Neural network-based learning algorithms are
now able to look at an image and describe

00:00:14.909 --> 00:00:20.660
what’s seen in this image, or even better,
the other way around, generating images from

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.660
a written description.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:27.089
You see here a set of results from BigGAN,
a state of the art image generation technique

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:31.730
and marvel at the fact that all of these images
are indeed synthetic.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.790
The GAN part of this technique abbreviates
the term Generative Adversarial Network - this

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:43.329
means a pair of neural networks that battle
each over time to master a task, for instance,

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:46.730
to generate realistic looking images when
given a theme.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.629
These detailed images are great, but, what
about generating video?

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:57.410
With the Dual Video Discriminator GAN, DVD-GAN
in short, DeepMind’s naming game is still

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:03.429
as strong as ever, it is now possible to create
longer and higher-resolution videos than was

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:11.910
previously possible, the exact numbers are
256x256 in terms of resolution and 48 frames,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.160
which is about 2 seconds.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.550
It also learned the concept of changes in
the camera view, zooming in on an object,

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.991
and understands that if someone draws something
with a pen, the ink has to remain on the paper

00:01:24.991 --> 00:01:26.450
unchanged.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:31.320
The Dual Discriminator part of the name reveals
one of the key ideas of the paper.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.220
In a classical GAN, we have a discriminator
network that looks at the images of the generator

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.470
network and critiques them.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:44.280
As a result, this discriminator learns to
tell fake and real images apart better, but

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.440
at the same time, provides ample feedback
for the generator neural network so it can

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.660
come up with better images.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.970
In this work, we have not one, but two discriminators,
one is called the spatial discriminator that

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:04.380
looks at just one image and assesses how good
it is structurally, while the second, temporal

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.039
discriminator critiques the quality of the
movement in these videos.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.680
This additional information provides better
teaching for the generator, which will in

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.349
return, be able to generate better videos
for us.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:22.390
The paper contains all the details that you
could possibly want to learn about this algorithm,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:27.790
in fact, let me give you two that I found
to be particularly interesting: one, it does

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.540
not get any additional information about where
the foreground and the background is, and

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.450
is able to leverage the learning capacity
of these neural networks to learn these concepts

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.040
by itself.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:45.680
And two, it does not generate the video frame
by frame sequentially, but it creates the

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.489
entire video in one go.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:50.450
That’s wild.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:57.569
Now, 256x256 is not a particularly high video
resolution, but if you have been watching

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:02.209
this series for a while, you are probably
already saying that two more papers down the

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:07.319
line, and we may be watching HD videos that
are also longer than we have the patience

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:08.359
to watch.

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:11.930
And all this through the power of machine
learning research.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.939
For now, let’s applaud DeepMind for this
amazing paper, and I can’t wait to have

00:03:16.939 --> 00:03:20.290
a look at more results and see some followup
works on it.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.629
What a time to be alive!

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:24.739
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

